## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a deadline. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` must cooperate with cancellation (recommended)

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Inside `work`, call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints.

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|-----------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. If `work` does a long blocking call without checking the token, it won't stop on time. |
| **Fire-and-forget work still runs** | The first version detects timeout via `WaitAsync`, but the abandoned `Task.Run` keeps executing. The work must check `CancellationToken` to actually stop. |
| **Uncooperative code can't be cancelled** | If `work` calls third-party code that ignores tokens, move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` — this is the only safe equivalent to the old `Thread.Abort`. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Forgetting to dispose leaks internal timers. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` ignores tokens** | Replace `Thread.Sleep(ms)` inside work with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or `Task.Delay(ms, token)`. |

### What was removed

| Old API | Replacement |
|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` (via constructor) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — no equivalent needed |
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(...)` |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `Task.WaitAsync(token)` |
